<Base lang="en" title="eSathi Login Not Working? Forgot Password or OTP Not Received — What to Do" description="eSathi login not working? Step-by-step fixes for edistrict forgot password, password reset, forgotten Login ID and OTP not received — the right website (esathi.up.gov.in), the correct recovery route, captcha and browser fixes, and when to use a CSC or the official helpdesk." path="/en/blog/esathi-login-forgot-password-otp/" altPath="/blog/esathi-login-forgot-password-otp/" faqs={faqs} updated="12 November 2026" datePublished="2026-11-12" dateModified="2026-11-12">
<div class="content-col"><h1 class="page-title">eSathi Login Problems: Password, OTP and Login ID — Every Fix</h1><p class="note">Note: This is an independent information guide — it is not the official portal (edistrict.up.gov.in).</p>
<p>You cannot log in to eSathi — the password is rejected, the OTP never arrives, or the login page will not open at all? Most people search for exactly this as “edistrict not working”, and the good news is that most of these problems are small and fixable — a mobile number typed where the Login ID belongs, one wrong letter in the captcha, or a delayed OTP. This article covers every case in order: quick checks first, then password and Login ID recovery, OTP fixes, page and error fixes, and finally the CSC and helpdesk routes.</p>
<figure class="blog-featured"><img src="/images/blog/esathi-login-forgot-password-otp-featured.webp" alt="eSathi login problems — password and OTP help (example illustration)" /><figcaption>Example illustration — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure>
<section class="box"><h2>Check these first — the most common causes</h2><p>Run through these four checks before starting recovery — half of all login problems end right here.</p><ul class="checklist"><li><strong>The right website:</strong> Citizen Login happens on the eSathi portal, <strong>esathi.up.gov.in</strong>. Do not enter credentials on a sponsored result or a lookalike private portal; check the domain spelling carefully.</li><li><strong>The Login ID is not your mobile number:</strong> the Login ID is the username you chose at registration. Many people keep typing their mobile number and seeing “invalid” — enter the ID you created when you registered.</li><li><strong>Browser and internet:</strong> use the Chrome browser on a stable connection. If the page stalls, clear the cache, turn off the ad-blocker and try a private (incognito) window. On mobile, switch on “Desktop site” mode.</li><li><strong>Fill the captcha carefully:</strong> watch the letter case (upper and lower) and keep the browser zoom at normal. If it is rejected, refresh the captcha and type it again.</li></ul></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Forgot your password — the correct reset route</h2><p>Forgetting the password is the most common problem, and the official route for it is on the portal itself. Do not try to get your password “recovered” by any person or website — that is not possible, and anyone who asks for your OTP to do it may be a fraud.</p><ol><li>Open the official eSathi login page — esathi.up.gov.in.</li><li>On the login page, choose the <strong>“Forgot Password”</strong> recovery option.</li><li>Give the details asked for — usually your Login ID and/or registered mobile.</li><li>Verify your identity with the <strong>OTP</strong> sent to the registered mobile.</li><li>Create a new, strong and <strong>unique password</strong> and note it somewhere safe. If the old password was also used on other websites, change it there too.</li></ol><div class="note">The portal interface can change over time. If the exact button name looks different, look for the recovery/help option on the official login page; this guide never collects anyone’s password or OTP.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>If you forgot the Login ID too</h2><p>Some people forget both the ID and the password — recover the ID first, then reset the password with the method above.</p><ol><li>If a <strong>“Forgot Login ID”</strong> option is available on the official login page, open it.</li><li>Fill in the account details and registered mobile asked for.</li><li>When you receive the OTP/SMS, note your ID and keep it written somewhere safe.</li></ol><div class="warn">No third party asks for an OTP to recover your ID. If anyone asks for an OTP on a call or message, do not share it — even if they claim to be the helpdesk or a CSC.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>OTP not received — what to do</h2><p>The OTP always goes to the <strong>registered mobile</strong> — the number you gave at registration. Entering a new or different number will not bring an OTP. If it still does not arrive, follow this order:</p><ol><li><strong>Wait a little.</strong> An OTP can be delayed; do not keep pressing resend immediately.</li><li><strong>Check the signal</strong> — on a weak network an SMS can get stuck. Restart the phone once if needed.</li><li><strong>Check blocked/spam SMS.</strong> Some phones push government SMS into a spam or blocked folder — check that folder along with the inbox, and see whether a government sender is on your blocked list.</li><li><strong>Check DND / message blocking.</strong> If an SMS-blocking setting is active on your number, the OTP can be stopped — check your number’s message settings.</li><li><strong>Resend after the cooldown.</strong> Wait a while before resending; repeated attempts make the problem worse.</li></ol><p>If the OTP still does not come, read the helpdesk section below. And always remember — never share your OTP, password or full Aadhaar number with anyone; the real helpdesk does not ask for an OTP either.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Login page not opening, or an error appears</h2><p>Most “not working” situations are in the table below — find your problem and its first fix.</p><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>First fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Invalid Login ID / Password</td><td>Enter the chosen Login ID, not the mobile number; then try a password reset.</td></tr><tr><td>Captcha incorrect</td><td>Refresh the captcha, check the letter case and keep the browser zoom at normal.</td></tr><tr><td>OTP not received</td><td>Check the registered mobile, signal and blocked SMS; resend after the cooldown.</td></tr><tr><td>Page not opening / server error</td><td>Use Chrome, clear the cache, turn off the ad-blocker and try an incognito window. On mobile, switch on “Desktop site” mode.</td></tr><tr><td>Session keeps expiring</td><td>The portal ends the session after a period of inactivity — keep all details ready beforehand and log in again.</td></tr><tr><td>Repeated login errors</td><td>Stop repeated attempts, try a private window, then contact the official helpdesk.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Sometimes the problem is temporary on the portal’s side — in that case, trying again after a while is the right move. And one useful point: even while login is stuck, your work does not stop completely — you can check your application status <a href="/en/status-verification/">with the application number, without login</a>. Once the login works, the next task for most people is taking the certificate PDF — the full route is in the <a href="/en/blog/esathi-download/">eSathi download guide</a>.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Registered mobile changed or stopped working</h2><p>OTP verification and alerts come only on the registered mobile, so keeping that number active is the easiest protection. If that number has stopped working or changed, recovery becomes difficult — in that situation, instead of workarounds or an agent’s help, contact the official helpdesk directly. No third party can recover your ID or password by asking for an OTP; beware of anyone who claims they can.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Help from a CSC / Jan Seva Kendra</h2><p>If you cannot solve it yourself, you can go to a nearby CSC (Jan Seva Kendra) and understand the process — the operator can show you the right page and the right order. But keep two things in mind: <strong>do not give your password or OTP even to the operator</strong> — log in with your own hands, and log out as soon as the work is done. For full details about centres, read our <a href="/en/csc-guide/">CSC guide</a>.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>When to contact the helpdesk</h2><p>If login still fails after all the steps above, your registered mobile has changed, or there is another problem with the account — contact the official helpdesk. Official district pages publish the Centre for e-Governance helpdesk: phone <strong>0522-2304706</strong> and email <strong>ceghelpdesk@gmail.com</strong>. When asking for help, describe your problem and give your application number — never give an OTP or password.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Safety rules — always remember these</h2><ul class="checklist"><li>Never share your OTP, password or full Aadhaar number with anyone — not even the helpdesk or a CSC operator.</li><li>Log in only on the official portal, esathi.up.gov.in; check the domain spelling every time.</li><li>Do not save your password on a shared computer, and log out when the work is done.</li><li>Beware of callers who ask you to install a remote-access app.</li><li>If you must send someone a screenshot of an error, first hide details like your Aadhaar, mobile and certificate number.</li><li>Keep your Login ID and password in a secure password manager or a private diary.</li></ul></section>
